+- **Layout**: Data structures for all pixels are stored in one memory pool in global GPU memory. Two arrays maintain the size and offset of the respective data structure in the pool for per-pixel access [S2].
 
 ### Texture Buffer Object for Block Types
-#### definition
-A technique to store block type colors in a Texture Buffer Object (TBO) and bind it as a texture to allow the vertex shader to look up colors based on the block type index stored in the vertex data [S3, unofficial].
-#### step
-- Define an array of 5 RGBA colors (each 4 floats) corresponding to block types: 0=air (black, alpha 0), 1=grass, 2=dirty, 3=water, 4=stone [S3, unofficial].
-- Upload the color array to the TBO using `tbo.data(colors)` [S3, unofficial].
-- Bind the TBO texture to texture unit 0 using `mogl::active_texture(0)` and `tbo_texture.bind()` [S3, unofficial].
-- Configure the TBO as a texture buffer with internal format `GL_R32F` using `tbo.tex_buffer(GL_R32F)` [S3, unofficial].
-- In the render loop, bind the TBO texture and set the uniform `texture_uniform_id` to 0 [S3, unofficial].
-#### layout
-- The TBO stores 5 colors, each as 4 floats (R32F format implies single channel, but the data is 4 floats per entry, likely interpreted as RGBA in the shader). The vertex data stores the block type as a `GLubyte` index into this texture [S3, unofficial].
+- **Definition**: A technique to store block type colors in a Texture Buffer Object (TBO) and bind it as a texture to allow the vertex shader to look up colors based on the block type index stored in the vertex data [S3, unofficial].
+- **Step**: Define an array of 5 RGBA colors (each 4 floats) corresponding to block types: 0=air (black, alpha 0), 1=grass, 2=dirty, 3=water, 4=stone [S3, unofficial]. Upload the color array to the TBO using `tbo.data(colors)` [S3, unofficial]. Bind the TBO texture to texture unit 0 using `mogl::active_texture(0)` and `tbo_texture.bind()` [S3, unofficial]. Configure the TBO as a texture buffer with internal format `GL_R32F` using `tbo.tex_buffer(GL_R32F)` [S3, unofficial]. In the render loop, bind the TBO texture and set the uniform `texture_uniform_id` to 0 [S3, unofficial].
+- **Layout**: The TBO stores 5 colors, each as 4 floats (R32F format implies single channel, but the data is 4 floats per entry, likely interpreted as RGBA in the shader). The vertex data stores the block type as a `GLubyte` index into this texture [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The techniques described in this family are derived from various implementations in volume rendering engines and research papers. The staging buffer approach is standard in Vulkan and modern OpenGL for efficient data transfer [S1, unofficial]. The GPU Data Layout structure is specific to VTK-based volume rendering implementations [S1, unofficial]. The Device Volume Snapshot and memory management strategies are drawn from Monte Carlo volume visualization research, specifically focusing on visibility optimization and efficient data structure pooling [S2]. The ClearView buffer construction is tailored for hexahedral mesh rendering [S1, unofficial]. The TBO technique is a general graphics programming pattern for indexed color lookups [S3, unofficial].
+The methods described in this family are derived from various implementations of volume rendering pipelines, including CUDA-based kernels [S2], OpenGL-based raycasting [S1, unofficial], and specialized mesh-based rendering techniques [S3, unofficial]. The use of staging buffers for volume upload is a common pattern in Vulkan and OpenGL to handle large data transfers efficiently [15388-volume-upload-via-staging-buffer.md]. The ClearView technique represents a specific approach to rendering hexahedral meshes by encoding topology into textures [2475-clearview-unified-buffer-construction.md].
 
